//! Navigation properties whose platform behavior needs more than a schema entry.

pub(super) fn note(name: &str) -> Option<&'static str> {
    Some(match name {
        "navigationInspector" => "  on a sheet route, true to show it as the inspector column of an expanded iOS split (LLP 1127 D14); on compact widths and other hosts it is a sheet.",
        "navigationSplit" => "  on a stack's root route (LLP 1116.004 D1.2). A wide window (900 px and up on the web) shows a stack whose root route is a list whose rows push beside its top, as a split view does, with nothing to write: a row pushes when its press does: a `link` to one of the app's routes, or a `button` or pressable whose action writes the router (`push`, `open`, `go`). A row's `image \"symbol:forward-chevron\"` (the disclosure an iOS list draws) is paint, which neither splits a list nor keeps one from splitting, and a list whose rows only select stays pushed. On hosts that infer from the router write alone, an action that goes back or opens a sheet can also qualify. Say `navigationSplit=\"none\"` for such a list. The list is a 320-wide column, its chevrons hidden and the row whose press pushed the detail marked; the top route is the detail beside it (a detail a row pushed has no Back), a `navigationPresentation=\"modal\"` route stays a sheet, and a narrow window pushes. Across a foldable browser's fold, two viewport segments side by side, the list fills the segment at its start and the detail the other, whatever the window's width or a number says. On iOS the split is UIKit's split view (LLP 1127), in an untabbed stack. On iPad a tab keeps its stack unless its first route says `navigationSplit=\"split\"`: its tab then holds the split, including across a tab switch. On a tab route write the literal \"split\" or \"none\", or leave it out. For both presentations use two tabs or two routes. An untabbed route also accepts a binding or a positive column width. An untabbed iPad root can ask the same way for a custom primary at the platform width. On iOS a compact width (an iPhone, the iPhone Duo folded) collapses it to one stack, the detail over the list with Back, and a wide one (an iPad, the iPhone Duo open) shows the columns as UIKit decides: the list beside the detail at UIKit's width (a grouped list, `appearance=\"auto\"`, as UIKit's sidebar list, the row of the detail's first route selected), the detail alone, or the list over it; under the agent's own chrome (`--chrome agent`) and on tvOS the stack pushes. The router is unchanged: a push from a row while a detail shows stacks it, so `replace` there to swap the detail. \"none\" keeps the stack pushed; a number is the column's width off a fold and splits a list the rule does not find. While nothing is selected the detail pane shows the platform's placeholder (\"No Selection\" on a Mac or an iPad, \"Select an item\" on the web, centred, in the secondary label's colour); \"placeholder\" on a box in the root route (an `EmptyState`) is your own instead, shown only there and then and laid out nowhere else. A root `tablist` left as the tabs recipe places it is a sidebar on the same window. docs/recipes/navigation-stack.md",
        _ => return None,
    })
}
